Test n = 1
LHS = 8³ + 6¹ = 518 = 74 × 7
∴ True for n = 1

Assume true for n = k
8^{2k+1} + 6^{2k−1} = 7P, where P is an integer.
Rearranging: 8^{2k+1} = 7P − 6^{2k−1}

Prove n = k + 1
WTP: 8^{2(k+1)+1} + 6^{2(k+1)−1} is divisible by 7.

LHS = 8^{2k+3} + 6^{2k−1}
= 64 × 8^{2k+1} + 36 × 6^{2k−1}
= 64(7P − 6^{2k−1}) + 36 × 6^{2k−1}, using assumption
= 64 × 7P − 64 × 6^{2k−1} + 36 × 6^{2k−1}
= 64 × 7P − 28 × 6^{2k−1}
= 7(64P − 4 × 6^{2k−1})
= 7Q, where Q is an integer

Since true for n = 1, by mathematical induction, true for all integers n ≥ 1.
